rfc6093.txt, rfc9406.txt, rfc6191.txt
    The texts of RFC 9293 (Transmission Control Protocol), RFC 7323 (TCP Extensions for High
    Performance), RFC 2018 (TCP Selective Acknowledgment Options), RFC 2883 (An Extension to the SACK
    Option), RFC 5961 (Improving TCP's Robustness to Blind In-Window Attacks), RFC 6298 (Computing TCP's
    Retransmission Timer), RFC 6528 (Defending against Sequence Number Attacks), RFC 5681 (TCP
    Congestion Control), RFC 6582 (The NewReno Modification to TCP's Fast Recovery Algorithm), RFC 6675
    (A Conservative Loss Recovery Algorithm Based on Selective Acknowledgment), RFC 9438 (CUBIC for Fast
    and Long-Distance Networks), RFC 6691 (TCP Options and Maximum Segment Size), RFC 3168 (The Addition
    of Explicit Congestion Notification to IP), RFC 8985 (The RACK-TLP Loss Detection Algorithm for
    TCP), RFC 7413 (TCP Fast Open), RFC 5482 (TCP User Timeout Option), RFC 6093 (On the Implementation
    of the TCP Urgent Mechanism), RFC 9406 (HyStart++) and RFC 6191 (Reducing the TIME-WAIT State Using
    TCP Timestamps), downloaded from https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/ on 2026-10-27.
    License: the IETF Trust's Legal Provisions Relating to IETF Documents.
    Used: the segment and option formats, the state machine and its MUST and SHLD requirements, the
    retransmission timer, the initial sequence numbers, congestion control and loss recovery, the
    checks against blind attacks, ECN, and the reuse of TIME-WAIT that the tests check. RFC 8985,
    RFC 7413, RFC 5482 and RFC 9406 are kept for what is not implemented, so that the reasons can be
    checked against them.

linux.txt
    Frames that the Linux kernel (7.0.14-linuxkit, of Docker Desktop on arm64, with the tools of Debian
    trixie) sent between two network namespaces on 2026-10-27, as seen by the client (192.0.2.1,
    2001:db8::1): connections over IPv4 and IPv6 with the options of RFC 7323 and RFC 2018, data both
    ways and a graceful close (Python echo servers); a connection to a closed port refused with a reset;
    32 KiB sent to a server that reads nothing for four seconds through a receive buffer of 2,048 bytes
    (a zero window and its probes); urgent data; keep-alives after one second of idleness; a bulk
    transfer whose every twelfth data segment the server drops after the capture point (iptables,
    "statistic --mode nth"), so that SACK, fast retransmit and recovery show; a connection with
    timestamps, SACK and window scaling turned off; and a connection that requests ECN (RFC 3168).
    Checksum offloading, TSO, GSO and GRO were turned off, so every segment is one the kernel built.
    Each frame is a line of hexadecimal after "# " lines with tcpdump's summary of it (`tcpdump -n -e -v`).
    Made by linux.sh, run as `docker run --rm --privileged -v "$PWD":/out debian:trixie sh /out/linux.sh`,
    and `python3 ../ethernet/linux.py tcp.pcap tcp.txt linux.txt`.
